# PCI ID fragment for the rocm-ernic emulated NIC subsystem
# (0x1dd8:0x100a, subsystem 0x1dd8:0x5400).
#
# Usually you do not need this. The device server presents a real,
# vendor-assigned pair, and current hwdata already lists 1dd8:100a as
# "DSC Serial Port Controller". With an up-to-date pci.ids database
# the subsystem line below renames that specific function to
# "ROCm Emulated RDMA NIC", which is what lspci will call it.
#
# The fragment is here for a guest whose pci.ids predates the
# registered subsystem entry. Merge the subsystem line under the
# existing 1dd8 / 100a device line in your system pci.ids; typical
# paths are /usr/share/hwdata/pci.ids and /usr/share/misc/pci.ids.
#
# Two things will bite you if you do it by hand:
#
#  - pci.ids is parsed sequentially, so the subsystem line must sit
#    inside the 100a device block. Appending it to the end of the file
#    puts it under the class section, where it never matches.
#  - If your pci.ids does not yet list the 1dd8 / 100a device at all,
#    the subsystem line has nowhere valid to attach. Update the guest's
#    hwdata package first.
#
# ernic_guest_setup does both checks for you (tasks/pci_ids.yml);
# keep ernic_guest_pciids_entry in its defaults in step with the
# line below. Note that update-pciids overwrites the system pci.ids
# and discards the edit; re-run the role after each update.
#
		1dd8  5400  ROCm Emulated RDMA NIC
